Ion Larranaga Azcue <ilarra@s21sec.com> writes:=0A=
=0A=
>I don't think it's necessary going to that degree of detail. For your=0A=
>specific first example, alert the alert "bad_certificate" is enough=0A=
=0A=
We already have error codes at about that level.  They're not enough to deb=
ug=0A=
any problems (I mean, "bad certificate" is only marginally better than the=
=0A=
canonical Ken Thompson Unix error message in which a giant "?" lights up in=
=0A=
front of you, "the experienced TLS developer will usually know what's wrong=
"),=0A=
thus the need for free-form text messages that tell you what the actual=0A=
problem is.=0A=
=0A=
>The problem I see with it is that it's hard for applications to automatica=
lly=0A=
>parse it,=0A=
=0A=
Applications don't need to parse it, it's an optional, opt-in=0A=
debugging/diagnostic facility to help developers sort out why a handshake i=
s=0A=
failing, not something that an application is meant to process.=0A=
=0A=
>I would first start trying to define an extended error reporting protocol=
=0A=
>using only numeric codes=0A=
=0A=
Please, go ahead and do so.  For that we'll probably need to start a new li=
st=0A=
to allow everyone to debate at length which error codes are needed and what=
=0A=
they should represent.  tls-error-bikeshedding@ietf.org seems to be a good=
=0A=
candidate name.=0A=
=0A=
Either that or say it's a UTF-8 string with free-form text, and we'll assum=
e=0A=
developers can somehow manage to figure the rest out themselves, as they ha=
ve=0A=
for pretty much every other situation in which free-form text error message=
s=0A=
are used.=0A=
=0A=
Peter.=0A=

